Subject: [PATCH 44/44] =?UTF-8?q?feat(chexc):=20T4=20=E2=80=94=20field=20i?=
 =?UTF-8?q?nitializers=20feed=20constructor=20inference,=20and=20'for-in'?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20demands=20its=20protocol?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two implicit invocations the pass did not see:

- An instance field initializer has no capability scope of its own, so its requirements are every
  evaluating constructor's ("What is inferred", point 3). They are now collected into each
  inference-eligible constructor, walked in that constructor's own scope, and the reporting pass
  reads a constructor's EFFECTIVE list rather than its written one -- otherwise inference would
  give the constructor exactly what the initializer needs and the initializer would still be
  reported. Before this, a class with a throwing field and a clause-free 'init' could not be
  written at all: the error landed on the initializer, and no 'try' at the construction site
  could reach it.

- 'for-in' calls 'iterator()' and 'next()' implicitly, and those calls do not exist as nodes until
  desugar -- which runs after this pass -- so the loop demanded nothing. They are synthesized as
  requirement sites, the same treatment the implicit 'close()' of try-with-resources gets.
  'forInKind' cannot gate this: it is decided in CHIR, so the subject's own members decide instead
  -- a range or a string reaches built-ins whose protocol members declare nothing.

The nullary-protocol-member lookup and the effective-list helper are now shared by 'close()',
'iterator()' and 'next()'.

Gate: CheckedExceptions + Sema/effect 171/171, std and stdx build clean at 'warn'.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
